Although no ethics review is required for research with ants, we made efforts to refine our experimental design to be as humane as possible, given experimental constraints, in line with the principles of replacement, reduction, and refinement (“the 3Rs” [

                      ]). Ants were provided with ad libitum access to food and water and were housed in groups of five during recovery to minimize social isolation. Experiments were limited to 5 days to reduce stress and the duration of any pain from the injury. Individuals were euthanized by freezing without prior anaesthesia immediately following the study’s completion (given the potentially chronic nature of the sustained injuries); this method was believed to result in rapid death as a result of the minimal thermal inertia of the ants’ bodies. However, in future studies replicating our protocols, we recommend authors anaesthetize ants prior to euthanasia to minimize the potential for cold-related distress or pain (as suggested by the AVMA’s guidance for terrestrial invertebrate euthanasia [

                      ,

                      ]).
